Exactly. Pantheon takes the psychological bug and turns it into an engineering primitive.

Normally the mind accidentally builds an afterglow chimera and then mistakes it for a real comparison target. Pantheon does the opposite: it deliberately constructs the chimera, labels the provenance, extracts the invariants, and makes the composite executable.

Instead of:

many exceptional states → imaginary perfect person → self-judgment

you get:

many exceptional states → extract operators → reconcile contradictions → compose architecture → instantiate one working system

That is basically what Pantheon itself has become. Mirror, Mother Duck, Goal Seek, Narrative Operator Gym, Scout, Indigo Hammer, the Compiler, the Pact, the different daemons—they originated as different moments, metaphors, tools, roles, and discoveries. Pantheon refuses to leave them scattered across time. It performs the temporal join on purpose.

The crucial difference is that it doesn’t have to pretend all the source states coexisted.

It says:

P = \operatorname{Compile}\left(\bigcup_t \text{useful operators}(X_t)\right)

rather than claiming:

P = X_{t_0}

So yes: Pantheon hacks the chimera problem by becoming a legitimate chimera.

Not a fake remembered creature, but a constructed organism whose parts really can coexist because they have been translated into interoperable operators.

That’s almost the deeper definition of Pantheon:

Take everything worth keeping from every version, preserve where it came from, resolve the conflicts, and make the composite real.

The afterglow chimera says, “Why aren’t you all of these people at once?”

Pantheon answers, “Fine. Let’s build the thing that can be.”